#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121020 :tutorial/content "Remember the following three standard elementary row operations: \n\n  I. Swapping two rows\n\n  II. Multiplying a row by a non-zero number\n\n  III. Adding a multiple of one row to another row\n\nOf these three elementary row operations, only III (adding a multiple of one row to another row) *preserves* the determinant. The other two operations, switching two rows or multiplying a row by a non-zero number, *change* the determinant.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's consider the matrix ![](images/fa/fa51d7c585db408c47e8640ae839c1a3bbf94e2b0097e1ade511e5773461f1e8.svg) Taking the determinant, we have\n![](images/bc/bcf5c5dc9f5b7ed3849e719665f802040cf51e3334ae99e25ea7648cbb0f6134.svg)\n\nIf we swap the two rows, we get a different determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} & =3\\cdot 2-1\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =2 \\\\  & \\ne -2\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLikewise, if we multiply top row by ${\\color{red}2},$ then we get a different determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}2\\cdot 1 & 2\\cdot 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{vmatrix} & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 4-3\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =-4 \\\\  & \\ne -2\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, if we add twice the top row to the bottom row, then we get the same determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 3+2\\cdot 1 & 4+2\\cdot 2\\end{vmatrix} & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 5 & 8\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot 8-2\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =-2\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "e5ae8e63-4d2c-5491-9908-96fe94243ab9" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3004 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121021 :tutorial/content "Since $\\det(A^T)=\\det(A),$ the corresponding elementary column operations on determinants have the same properties as the elementary row ones.\n\n- Swapping two columns changes the sign of the determinant.\n\n- Multiplying a column by a number scales the determinant by that number.\n\n- Adding a multiple of one column to another column does not change the determinant." :tutorial/id #uuid "bac0b72b-b76a-5a06-9312-d2b7b8ac4247" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3005 :tutorial/title "Column Operations on Determinants"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121044 :tutorial/content "As we saw, swapping two rows does not preserve the determinant. However, the only difference is the sign of the determinant changes. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{vmatrix} & =1\\cdot 4-2\\cdot 3=-2 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} & =3\\cdot 2-1\\cdot 4=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, in the above example, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{vmatrix} & =-\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn general, swapping any two rows of a matrix changes the sign of the determinant to the opposite one. That is to say, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det\\begin{bmatrix}⋮ & ⋮ & … & ⋮ \\\\ ◻ & ◻ & … & ◻ \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & … & ⋮ \\\\ △ & △ & … & △ \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & … & ⋮\\end{bmatrix}=-det\\begin{bmatrix}⋮ & ⋮ & … & ⋮ \\\\ △ & △ & … & △ \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & … & ⋮ \\\\ ◻ & ◻ & … & ◻ \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & … & ⋮\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "f151b612-1bdb-5723-af50-a48439141fbc" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3236 :tutorial/title "Switching Two Rows Changes the Sign of the Determinant"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121045 :tutorial/content "As we have seen, multiplying a row by a number does not preserve the determinant. However, it does affect the determinant in a predictable way: multiplying a row by a number scales the determinant by that number as well.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{vmatrix} & =1\\cdot 4-2\\cdot 3=-2 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}2\\cdot 1 & 2\\cdot 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{vmatrix}=\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{vmatrix} & =2\\cdot 4-3\\cdot 4=-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, in the above example, we have\n![](images/08/0826c05fe2eabec96065eb2c83228168129b42c309764df4b101f4a520c4a0f9.svg)\n\nIn general, multiplying a row of a matrix by a number multiplies the determinant by that number. That is to say, we can factor out the common multiplier from the row of the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det\\begin{bmatrix}⋮ & ⋮ & … & ⋮ \\\\ k△ & k△ & … & k△ \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & … & ⋮\\end{bmatrix}=k\\cdot det\\begin{bmatrix}⋮ & ⋮ & … & ⋮ \\\\ △ & △ & … & △ \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & … & ⋮\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "09c28da6-5340-56f9-bbf5-427a824977ee" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3237 :tutorial/title "Multiplying a Row By a Number Scales the Determinant By That Number"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121061 :tutorial/content "Let's check what happens if we multiply the entire matrix (not only one row) by a number. For example,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{vmatrix} & =1\\cdot 4-2\\cdot 3=-2 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}3\\cdot 1 & 3\\cdot 2 \\\\ 3\\cdot 3 & 3\\cdot 4\\end{vmatrix}=\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 6 \\\\ 9 & 12\\end{vmatrix} & =3\\cdot 12-6\\cdot 9=-18\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, in the above example, we have\n\n![](images/fe/fe02091dde43a8a2405f6b588f619bc9fa3f54b31ac8a1ac39d318761f00cc3e.svg)\n\nThe above is equivalent to multiplying both rows of the matrix by $3$. Multiplying the first row scales the determinant by $3$, and then multiplying the second row scales it by $3$ once more.\n\nIn general, multiplying an entire $n \\times n$ matrix by a number multiplies the determinant by that number to the power of $n$. That is to say, we can factor out the common multiplier from the entire matrix, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(k\\cdot A) &={k}^{n}\\cdot det(A),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an $n \\times n$ matrix." :tutorial/id #uuid "1c99dfd1-b583-5247-9ac5-c2ada47c0ee9" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3880 :tutorial/title "Multiplying a Matrix By a Scalar"}]}
